 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** The basic voxel iteration is $O(r^3)$ where $r$ is the radius in voxels, as it visits every voxel in the bounding box [S1, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory for the loop variables, plus $O(k)$ for `gib_positions` where $k$ is the number of removed voxels [S1, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The operation is efficient for real-time destructible terrain. The cost is dominated by memory access patterns in the voxel chunk. Clamping the bounding box ensures no out-of-bounds checks are needed inside the loop, optimizing performance [S1, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory for loop variables. Returning `gib_positions` requires $O(k)$ memory where $k$ is the number of removed voxels [S3, unofficial]. Source [S1, unofficial] returns only the removal count.
+*   **Performance:** The operation is efficient for real-time destructible terrain. Clamping the bounding box ensures no out-of-bounds checks are needed inside the loop, optimizing performance [S1, S3, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, deterministic, and provides explicit feedback on destroyed voxels (positions) for visual effects (gibs) [S1, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** It is a binary operation (solid/air) and does not support partial destruction or smooth erosion without additional logic. It requires the caller to manage the coordinate transformation (world to local/grid) if the chunk uses a local coordinate system [S3, unofficial].
